In this lesson we'll look at linear and linear simultaneous equations, which is just a fancy way of saying two straight-line equations that share the same pair of x and y values. First up is substitution. You pick one equation and rearrange it so a variable is written in terms of the other, then you drop that expression into the second equation. That leaves you with a single variable to solve, and you plug your answer back in to find the other one. Next is elimination. Here, you multiply one or both equations by a number so the coefficients on one variable match but have opposite signs. When you add the equations together, that variable disappears, and again you're left with one variable to solve, then back-substitute. As a bonus check, you can graph both lines and confirm they cross at exactly the point you found. Now let's see it in action.